+- Above `pub(crate) fn repository_scope(` the block is exactly its own 3 lines, beginning
+  `/// The same addressing rule as [\`addressable_scope\`] for records that belong to the local` — no `# Errors` heading anywhere in its context (FR-002).
+- Above `fn synthesize_schema()` the block begins `/// Closed like every other schema here. \`fixture\` and \`route\` are both optional and the route`.
+- Above `fn assign_schema()` there is **no** `///` context line at all (blank line after the previous item's closing brace) — the borrowed text is gone (FR-003).
+
+## Scenario 3 — No duplication, no loss (SC-001, SC-002)
+
+```powershell
+$files = @('apps\cli\src\commands\execution\mod.rs', 'apps\cli\src\commands\mcp\tools.rs')
+foreach ($f in $files) {
+  (Select-String -Path $f -Pattern '^\s*///').Count   # must equal the Step 0 count for this file
+}
+(Select-String -Path $files[0] -Pattern 'The ONE scope the').Count        # expected 1
+(Select-String -Path $files[0] -Pattern '# Errors').Count                 # expected: same as Step 0 file count, all pre-existing
+(Select-String -Path $files[1] -Pattern 'exactly one door').Count         # expected 1
+(Select-String -Path $files[1] -Pattern 'Closed like every other schema here').Count   # expected 1
+```
+
+**Expected outcome**: per-file `///` totals unchanged from Step 0 (nothing added or removed); each
+block sentinel appears exactly once (no copy left at the old position, no orphan produced).
+
+## Scenario 4 — Cross-references still resolve (SC-003; contract §2)
+
+Static check (no build): the moved blocks remain in the modules where their link targets are
+declared — `[`Failure`]` targets the struct at `execution/mod.rs` (baseline line 80) and
+`[`addressable_scope`]` targets the function now directly below its own block; both stay
+same-module after the move, and the tools.rs block uses plain backtick code spans, so no link
+target changed module. Verify no link syntax was altered by the diff itself:
+
+```powershell
+git diff -U0 -- apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs |
+  Select-String -Pattern '^[+-]' | Where-Object { $_ -match '\[[^\]]*\]' -and $_ -notmatch '^[+-]\s*///' }
+```
+
+**Expected outcome**: prints nothing — every changed line carrying bracket-link syntax is a `///`
+line already accounted for by Scenario 1.
+
+## Scenario 5 — Behavior neutrality (SC-004)
+
+The change touches no executable code; equivalence of reachable checks is the neutrality proof.
+
+- **S5a (always run; no compilation)**:
+  ```powershell
+  cargo +1.97.1 fmt -p graphhelm-cli -- --check; echo "exit=$LASTEXITCODE"
+  ```
+  **Expected**: exit 0 both before (Step 0 era) and after the change — the relocated lines were
+  already rustfmt-stable and stayed unindented and unwrapped.
+- **S5b (optional secondary observer; compiles the one touched crate)**:
+  ```powershell
+  cargo +1.97.1 doc -p graphhelm-cli --no-deps --locked 2>&1 | Select-String -Pattern 'unresolved link|warning' | Sort-Object -Unique
+  ```
+  Run before and after the implementation; **expected**: identical output (no newly unresolved
+  intra-doc links, no new warnings). The affected items are non-public, so their text is not on
+  published rustdoc pages — this observer covers link resolution, while Scenarios 2–4 cover the
+  attachment promise directly (research R3/R5).
+
+## Out of scope for validation here
+
+Per spec edge cases: a sweep for *other* misattached blocks across the repository is out of scope;
