+# Research: Restore Displaced Documentation Comments
+
+**Feature**: [spec.md](spec.md) · **Plan**: [plan.md](plan.md) · **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+**Baseline**: clean working tree at head `25b34ed` (only untracked paths: `.specify/`, `specs/`).
+All line numbers below are from that baseline. No `NEEDS CLARIFICATION` item in the plan remains
+unresolved after this phase.
+
+## R1 — Block attribution: which function owns each displaced block?
+
+- **Decision**: Confirmed spec assumption A1 against source.
+  - `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs` lines 208–218 (11 lines, beginning "The ONE scope the
+    `execution` verbs can address for a given execution id.", containing the `# Errors` subsection
+    "[`Failure`] when `execution` is not a valid identifier.") belongs to `addressable_scope`.
+    Its own block, lines 219–221 (3 lines, "The same addressing rule as [`addressable_scope`] for
+    records that belong to the local repository rather than to one execution stream …"), belongs to
+    `repository_scope`.
+  - `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs` lines 741–744 (4 lines, citing `fixture` and `route` and
+    the server's `synthesize` route) belongs to `synthesize_schema`, not to `assign_schema`.
+- **Rationale**: measured against each function's parameter/failure surface (the ground-truth test
+  named by the spec):
+  - `addressable_scope(execution: &str) -> Result<RepositoryScope, Failure>` (line 230) is the only
+    one of the two scoping helpers that takes an `execution` argument and can return a failure
+    (`ExecutionId::parse` mapped to the argument diagnostic). The block's error subsection names
+    exactly that parameter. `repository_scope() -> RepositoryScope` (line 222) takes no parameters
+    and cannot fail — its body parses compile-time constants under `expect` — so an error contract
+    about an `execution` argument cannot describe it.
+  - `synthesize_schema()` (tools.rs line 756) declares the `fixture` and `route` fields (lines
+    772–781); `assign_schema()` (line 745) declares only `executionId`, `node`, `actorId`. A block
+    stating "`fixture` and `route` are both optional" cannot be `assign_schema`'s description.
+  - A file-wide scan of `///` lines in tools.rs found no separate block describing
+    `assign_schema` anywhere; per A2 the merge inserted the displaced block above `assign_schema`,
+    so `assign_schema` ends the change undocumented — which is what the spec describes ("the
+    function it describes is undocumented"; after the fix "the first function's documentation no
+    longer includes it").
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Attribute by position (block sits above the function)* — rejected: that is precisely the
+    state the merge created and it misdescribes both functions.
+  - *Split the contiguous 15-line run into "both blocks describe both functions"* — rejected: the
+    `# Errors` subsection and the `execution` citations occur once, verbatim, and tie to a single
+    function's signature; the spec's edge-case section requires the intervening function to keep
+    precisely its own three-line block.
+
+## R2 — Displacement shape: exact block boundaries in the execution file
+
+- **Decision**: In `execution/mod.rs` the displaced run and `repository_scope`'s own block are
+  one contiguous `///` region (lines 208–221, no separating blank line), currently rendering as
+  one documentation block on `repository_scope`. The fix moves exactly lines 208–218; lines 219–221
+  stay where they are. After the move the file reads, in order: blank line → 3-line block →
+  `pub(crate) fn repository_scope` → body → closing brace → blank line (existing line 229) →
+  11-line block → `pub(crate) fn addressable_scope`.
+- **Rationale**: rustdoc attaches a run of consecutive inner doc-comment lines to the immediately
+  following item. The measured layout (baseline lines 207–230: blank 207, docs 208–221, fn 222,
+  fn 230 with only a blank line between) shows the boundary between the two logical blocks sits
+  after the `# Errors` line (218) — the 219th line reopens with a complete sentence about a
+  different subject ("The same addressing rule as [`addressable_scope`] for records that belong to
+  the local repository…"), which matches the spec's edge case ("contiguous … only the displaced
+  portion moves; the intervening function keeps precisely its own three-line block"). Spacing
+  around all neighboring items is therefore unchanged automatically: no blank line is added or
+  removed anywhere.
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Insert a separating blank line inside the comment run before splitting* — rejected: it would
+    add a line (FR-005 forbids additions) and would alter spacing around the block (edge case on
+    whitespace discipline).
+
+## R3 — Rustdoc attachment semantics used as the correctness model
+
+- **Decision**: Correctness is defined operationally as source-position correctness: a
+  documentation block renders on function X if and only if its consecutive `///` lines end on the
+  line directly above X's item header (modulo the standard blank-line-before-block spacing that
+  already exists). Both fixes are "cut a contiguous run and paste it with no surrounding-byte
+  change directly above the owner's signature".
+- **Rationale**: the observable promise (SC-001, "blocks render on their intended functions")
+  reduces to that positional rule because rustdoc's attachment is purely local and lexical; no
+  configuration or attribute in either file (e.g. `#[doc(hidden)]`, doc aliases) changes
+  attachment for these items — verified by inspection of the item headers at baseline lines 222,
+  230, 745, 756. The affected items are `pub(crate)`/private, so their rendered text surfaces in
+  hover help / IDE tooling and any rustdoc pass rather than in the published public pages; position
+  inspection plus, optionally, a `cargo doc` pass covers the same attachment decision.
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Proof via published rustdoc HTML pages only* — rejected as the primary observer: the items are
+    not public, so the pages do not expose them; it would be a weaker observer than source
+    inspection, not a stronger one.
+
+## R4 — Cross-reference resolution after the move
+
+- **Decision**: All references inside moved blocks keep resolving identically after the move.
+  - Moved block (execution/mod.rs 208–218) contains intra-doc links `[`Failure`]` and
+    `[`addressable_scope`]`; the staying block (219–221) contains `[`addressable_scope`]`. Targets:
+    `Failure` is declared in the same module (mod.rs line 80) and `addressable_scope` is the moved
+    block's own new neighbor (same module). The move is within the module, so resolution paths are
+    unchanged (FR-006, SC-003).
+  - Moved block (tools.rs 741–744) mentions `serve::routes:: synthesize` and `start_schema` inside
+    backticks (plain code spans, not `[...]` intra-doc links); `start_schema` is in the same file
+    (line 424). No link target changes module either way.
+- **Rationale**: intra-doc links resolve by name within the defining scope of the documenting
+  item; same-module relocation preserves the scope, so no reference can break and none can newly
+  resolve (SC-003's second half: documented text of the remaining items is unchanged).
+- **Alternatives considered**: rewriting or adding links to improve discoverability — rejected:
+  any textual edit violates FR-005.
+
+## R5 — Verification surface: smallest adequate proof
+
+- **Decision**: Primary observers, in ascending cost:
+  1. Diff discipline over the two files (git): changed-line census proves FR-004/FR-005 and
+     SC-002 (every +/- content line is a `///` line; each moved line appears verbatim elsewhere in
+     the diff; exactly two paths modified; no other working-tree modification beyond this feature's
+     artifacts).
+  2. Attachment inspection (quickstart S2/S3): the line directly above each of the four signatures
+     equals the expected final/first doc line, and sentinel strings appear exactly once in each
+     file — proves SC-001 and that no block was duplicated or lost.
+  3. Optional equivalence checks for SC-004 (behavior neutrality): crate-scoped
+     `cargo +1.97.1 fmt -p graphhelm-cli -- --check` before/after (no compilation) and, if a build
+     is acceptable at implementation time, `cargo +1.97.1 doc -p graphhelm-cli --no-deps --locked`
+     comparing warnings before/after. No new automated tests are justified: there is no executable
+     behavior change, and adding a test that froze comment positions would be a proxy the Keel
+     rules ("smallest adequate observer", "reject … checks that merely freeze incidental source
+     spelling") say not to add.
+- **Rationale**: Journey-Proven Development obliges selecting the smallest proof strong enough for
+  the obligation and risk; the obligations here are textual and positional, so text/position
+  observers are the direct ones. HTTP-style proxies are absent by construction (spec A4).
+- **Alternatives considered**:
+  - *Add unit tests asserting doc-comment positions* — rejected: they would assert against
+    incidental source spelling/positions of an unchanging property, exactly the class of tests the
+    repository rules reject.
+  - *Full workspace gate* — rejected as overkill for two comment moves in one crate; reserved for
+    the delivery process outside this feature's proof.
+
+## R6 — Byte fidelity of the relocation on this platform
+
+- **Decision**: Treat the move as byte-exact at the git level: both files match
+  `.gitattributes` `*.rs text eol=lf`, so git compares LF-normalized content; the relocated lines
+  must be written back with their exact characters (indentation included: all moved lines begin
+  at column 0 — top-level `///` items, no nested indentation exists in these runs) and the tool
+  performing the edit must preserve the files' existing line endings. `git diff` against the
+  baseline is the authoritative check; raw working-copy byte counts are not (CRLF normalization on
+  Windows checkouts makes raw comparisons noisy without changing what git sees).
+- **Rationale**: FR-005 requires verbatim lines and the repo pins `.rs` to LF, which gives a stable
+  comparison basis independent of the machine editing; the diff census (quickstart S1) then needs
+  no tolerance logic.
+- **Alternatives considered**: byte-for-byte whole-file hash comparison of untouched regions —
+  useful as an extra assertion but subsumed by the hunk-level git diff, which also attributes
+  every change to a line.
