+# Feature Specification: Restore Displaced Documentation Comments
+
+**Feature Branch**: `[001-bench]`
+
+**Created**: 2026-10-26
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "A merge inserted `repository_scope` between `addressable_scope`'s doc block and `addressable_scope` in `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs`, and `assign_schema` between `synthesize_schema`'s doc block and `synthesize_schema` in `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs`. rustdoc now attaches those docs to the wrong items. Move each displaced doc block back directly above its own function. Change comments only: no code, no reformatting, no other files."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Each scoping helper documents only itself (Priority: P1)
+
+A maintainer opens the generated documentation (rendered docs or hover help) for the two
+execution-scoping helpers in the CLI's execution command module. One of the helper's
+documentation blocks was separated from its function when a merge inserted another function
+between the block and the function. Today the block renders under the *wrong* function, so a
+reader sees an error contract that belongs to a sibling function and misses the block that
+actually belongs to the one they are reading. After this change, the displaced block sits
+directly above its own function, and each of the two helpers displays exactly the text written
+for it — nothing more, nothing less.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the core defect. A documentation block describing function A's
+parameters and error behavior rendered on function B is actively misleading — readers make
+wrong assumptions about which values can fail and how. Restoring attachment on the first file
+delivers the primary value on its own.
+
+**Independent Test**: Inspect the documented text of each affected function after the change:
+the previously displaced function shows its full block (including its error section), the
+function standing between them shows only its own pre-existing block, and no block is duplicated
+or lost.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the execution-scoping module in which one function's documentation block was
+   separated from the function by an intervening item, **When** the displaced block is moved
+   back directly above its own function, **Then** the function's rendered documentation contains
+   exactly its original block — body and error section intact — and the intervening function's
+   documentation contains only the block that always described it.
+2. **Given** a cross-reference inside a documentation block that points at a sibling item,
+   **When** the block is moved within the same module, **Then** the reference still resolves to
+   the same target and no new broken references appear.
+3. **Given** the change is complete, **When** any reader compares the documented set of lines
+   before and after, **Then** every line is preserved verbatim — moved as whole blocks, never
+   reworded, added, or removed.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - The schema builder documents its own routing rule (Priority: P2)
+
+An integrator reads the documentation for the MCP tool-schema builders in the CLI's MCP module.
+The block explaining the synthesize tool's field options and its "exactly one door" routing rule
+currently renders on an unrelated, adjacent schema builder, while the function it describes is
+undocumented. After this change, the block sits directly above the synthesize schema function,
+and the adjacent builder no longer borrows its description.
+
+**Why this priority**: Same defect class as P1 in the second named location; fixing this file
+completes the reported damage and is independently valuable and testable.
+
+**Independent Test**: Inspect the documented text of the two adjacent schema-builder functions:
+the one whose fields the block names now carries it, the other shows no borrowed text, and no
+block is duplicated or lost.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** two adjacent schema-builder functions where the second's documentation block was
+   inserted above the first, **When** the block is moved directly above the second function,
+   **Then** the second function's documentation matches its original block verbatim and the first
+   function's documentation no longer includes it.
+2. **Given** the relocated block still references the shared "exactly one door" server-side rule,
+   **When** the move keeps it in the same module, **Then** all of its cross-references still
+   resolve and none of them break.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - The repair proves it changed nothing but comments (Priority: P3)
+
+A reviewer evaluates the change and needs evidence that it is behavior-neutral before trusting it.
+The diff must show only documentation-comment lines moving position — no signatures, no bodies,
+no reformatting of untouched lines, no files beyond the two named locations.
+
+**Why this priority**: The trust property that makes this low-risk change acceptable; it can be
+verified against either fix slice independently, and it protects reviewers of both files.
+
+**Independent Test**: Compare the before/after state of the working tree: every changed line is a
+documentation comment line verbatim-relocated, the non-comment lines are byte-for-byte identical,
+and no path outside the two named files appears in the change.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** the completed change, **When** the reviewer inspects the diff, **Then** every added
+   or removed line is a documentation comment line, each moved line appears unchanged elsewhere
+   in the diff, and no executable code line differs.
+2. **Given** the completed change, **When** the reviewer lists the modified paths, **Then**
+   exactly the two named source files are present.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- The execution-scoping file's displaced block and the intervening function's own block are
+  contiguous (no separating blank comment): only the displaced portion moves; the intervening
+  function keeps precisely its own three-line block, which already referenced the sibling — so
+  the move must not create a duplicate reference pair or orphan the block.
+- The displaced block in the scoping file ends with an error-contract subsection tied to that
+  function's single string parameter; the subsection must travel with the whole block and must
+  not be left stranded on the intervening function (which takes no parameter and cannot fail).
+- The relocated block in the MCP module cites field names that exist only on one of the two
+  adjacent functions; ownership is decided by those citations, so the move must land the block on
+  the function that actually declares the cited fields.
+- Whitespace discipline: moving a block must not re-indent, re-wrap, or re-flow any line, and
+  must not alter spacing around neighboring functions.
+- No third location shares this defect: scope is bounded to the two merge displacements reported;
+  a sweep finding other misattached blocks is out of scope for this feature.
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: System MUST restore the documentation block displaced in
+  `apps/cli/src/commands/execution/mod.rs` directly above `addressable_scope`, so the function's
+  rendered documentation again contains its full original block, including its error section.
+- **FR-002**: In the same file, `repository_scope` MUST retain exactly its own pre-existing
+  documentation block (the one referencing `addressable_scope` and describing records that belong
+  to the local repository rather than to one execution stream) directly above itself, with none of
+  the displaced block's lines remaining attached to it.
+- **FR-003**: System MUST move the documentation block currently sitting above `assign_schema` in
+  `apps/cli/src/commands/mcp/tools.rs` directly above `synthesize_schema`, since the block
+  describes the synthesize schema's fields and the server-side "exactly one door" routing rule.
+- **FR-004**: The change MUST be comment-only: no function signature, body, or any other code may
+  change; no line outside the moved blocks may be reformatted; no file other than the two named
+  files may be touched.
+- **FR-005**: Documentation lines MUST be preserved verbatim and moved as whole contiguous blocks:
+  no line may be added, removed, reworded, split, or re-wrapped.
+- **FR-006**: Every cross-reference contained in a moved block MUST continue to resolve to the
+  same target after the move (all moved blocks remain in the same module as their targets).
+
+### Key Entities *(include if feature involves data)*
+
+- **Documentation Block**: the unit of work — a run of consecutive documentation-comment lines
+  that belongs to exactly one function. Attributes: verbatim text (immutable in this change),
+  intended owning function, current (wrong) position and corrected position.
+- **Owning Function**: the internal helper whose rendered documentation is the observable
+  outcome. Attributes: identity (name and file), parameter/failure surface (the ground truth used
+  to attribute a block), documented text after the fix.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: 2 of 2 (100%) displaced documentation blocks render on their intended functions
+  after the change, with 0 blocks remaining attached to a wrong item.
+- **SC-002**: 100% of the changed lines are documentation-comment lines that are verbatim copies
+  of pre-change lines; 0 non-comment lines differ and 0 files outside the two named files change.
+- **SC-003**: 0 newly unresolvable cross-references across the two files after the move, and the
+  documented text of the remaining items is unchanged.
+- **SC-004**: Any verification available for the touched areas yields identical results before
+  and after the change, demonstrating the change is behavior-neutral.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- **A1 (block attribution)**: Ownership of each disputed block is determined from its content:
+  the block stating "the ONE scope the `execution` verbs can address" with an error section about
+  an `execution` argument belongs to `addressable_scope`, because only that function takes an
+  `execution` parameter and can fail; the block referencing `[addressable_scope]` and describing
+  local-repository records (e.g. the Dreams shadow ledger) belongs to `repository_scope`. In the
+  MCP module, the block citing `fixture`/`route` fields and the server's `synthesize` route
+  belongs to `synthesize_schema`, because those fields exist only there.
+- **A2 (displacement shape)**: The merge inserted the intervening function together with its own
+  (correct) documentation block; therefore correcting the defect moves exactly one block per
+  file and leaves the intervening function's own block untouched, keeping existing function order.
+- **A3 (verbatim relocation)**: The documentation prose is treated as authoritative; the correct
+  remedy is relocation, not rewording, so no line content is edited.
+- **A4 (verification surface)**: Because no executable logic changes, proof is observation of the
+  rendered/documented text, the diff (comment-only, two files), and equivalence of any reachable
+  automated checks; no new runtime behavior exists to prove.
+- **A5 (governance substitution)**: This checkout contains no `/memory/constitution.md`; per the
+  run instructions, the repository-root `AGENTS.md` file serves as the governance reference for
+  this specification, recorded here as the required substitution.
+- **A6 (naming)**: The feature directory is fixed by the run to `specs/001-bench`; the short name
+  generated from the description (`restore-displaced-docs`) informs the title and branch label
+  only, not the directory path.
